WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
GOOGLE MAPS:
I’m sure you’ve all used Google Maps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.080
Whether it’s to get directions to a new restaurant&nbsp;
or stalk someone’s house through street view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.720
we’re all familiar with the tremendous resource&nbsp;
that is Google Maps. Ssomething that most of us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.800
likely take for granted is that just like all&nbsp;
of Google’s other products, Maps is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.120
free to use for as long as you want. This sort of&nbsp;
freemium model isn’t too surprising with search or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.720
email where everything you need is virtual, but&nbsp;
maps is a pretty grueling business that requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.320
a lot of physical resources. From the dozens of&nbsp;
satellites and hundreds of street view cars that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.760
constantly monitor and take pictures of the&nbsp;
Earth to all the servers and supercomputers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.120
required to complete millions hours of processing&nbsp;
to seamlessly stitch together petabytes of data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.320
And let’s not forget about all the hours spent&nbsp;
on blurring out license plates, faces, houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.400
and other personally identifiable information.&nbsp;
Clearly, there’s no question that Google Maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.160
takes a lot of resources to keep functional and&nbsp;
up to date. And while its value proposition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.440
Google wasn’t too clear in the early days, Maps&nbsp;
has grown to be one of Google’s most lucrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.840
businesses. In fact, Morgan Stanley estimates&nbsp;
that Google Maps will pull in over $11 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.680
in revenue in 2023 alone. For perspective,&nbsp;
that’s about the same as TikTok. And if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.880
valued Google Maps the same way as Alphabet,&nbsp;
Google Maps itself would be worth roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:31.600
$62 billion. That’s about the same as Mercedes&nbsp;
Benz and more than Volkswagen and PayPal. Heck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:39.080
that’s enough to be a top 250 company by itself.&nbsp;
But how does Google even monetize Maps? They don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.760
show video ads or search ads, and there doesn’t&nbsp;
seem to be much opportunity to collect and sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.920
our data based on just our Google Maps usage.&nbsp;
Well, it turns out that Google has come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:54.880
some pretty creative ways to indirectly monetize&nbsp;
Maps while staying surprisingly ethical. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:08.097
here’s the insane rise of Google Maps&nbsp;
and how Google Maps makes so much money.

00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:09.920
AN UNDERWHELMING START:
Taking a look back, the story of Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.960
Maps takes as back 20 years to 2003. Like many&nbsp;
of Google’s products, Maps wasn’t something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.080
Google created in house, it was actually something&nbsp;
that they bought from 4 men named Lars Rasmussen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.120
Jens Rasmussen, Noel Gordon, and Stephen Ma.&nbsp;
Together, they had founded a startup called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.360
Where 2 Technologies in Sydney Australia. The&nbsp;
idea for the platform was pretty simple: create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:38.000
a desktop map program. But it turns out that this&nbsp;
was a lot easier said than down and pretty soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.960
they needed a lot more resources leading them&nbsp;
to meet up with Google in late 2004. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.920
Google wasn’t actually convinced about Where 2&nbsp;
Technologies until the founders suggested the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.920
idea of converting the desktop application into&nbsp;
a web platform that could be accessed much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.080
easily. With that, Google would agree to take a&nbsp;
flier on the company for an undisclosed amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.480
which was likely well below $50 million. Where&nbsp;
2 Technologies wasn’t the only map based company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.800
that Google purchased in 2004 though. Around&nbsp;
the same time, they would purchase two other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.840
map companies called Keyhole and ZipDash.&nbsp;
Like Where 2 Technologies, Keyhole was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.600
desktop application unlike Where 2 Technologies,&nbsp;
Keyhole was more focused on the macro scale. Aka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:26.520
viewing the earth and various countries as opposed&nbsp;
to specific streets and directions. I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.800
you’d be surprised to hear that this is what would&nbsp;
turn into Google Earth, and Google got this entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:40.080
company for just $35 million. But, in terms of&nbsp;
value, nothing comes close to ZipDash. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.720
ZipDash might be Google’s most bang for&nbsp;
the buck acqusition of all time as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.280
were able to purchase the company for just $2&nbsp;
million. What did ZipDash do you ask? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.600
they specialized in using location data from&nbsp;
mobile phones to create approximate real time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.600
traffic data. This tech would be underappreciated&nbsp;
and under used by Google for several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.280
but once smartphones started taking off, all&nbsp;
of that would completely change. But anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.000
going back to 2004, after all the acquisitions&nbsp;
were done, Google would spend a few months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.080
refining the technologies before finally launching&nbsp;
Google Maps on February 18, 2005. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.640
Google Maps would get Slashdotted the night before&nbsp;
its launch. If you also don’t what that means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.600
it basically means that Google Maps went viral&nbsp;
on a forum the day before launch which led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:34.960
an explosive launch right out the gate but this&nbsp;
traffic didn’t last. You see, for a lot of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.680
myself included, 2005 probably sounds like&nbsp;
the stone ages when it comes to the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.760
but it turns out that there were already&nbsp;
some pretty great mapping solutions on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.880
market before Google Maps specifically Yahoo&nbsp;
Maps and MapQuest. And while Google Maps was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.720
superior to these offerings from day one, that&nbsp;
by itself was by no means enough displace the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:01.160
market leaders. For most people, Yahoo Maps and&nbsp;
MapQuest already accomplished what they needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.720
and they had no reason to use Google Maps instead.&nbsp;
So, the general consensus for year 1 was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:13.440
Google Maps did just okay. It pulled in a decent&nbsp;
amount of traffic but nothing too crazy. Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.840
could’ve easily just called it here and kept Maps&nbsp;
a small side business, but they were determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:29.480
to make Maps much more than just that. And as&nbsp;
such, they would scrap everything and start over.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.240
A COMPLETE DO OVER:
Given the underwhelming response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.800
Google would decide to do a complete rewrite of&nbsp;
Google Maps. In turns out that while Google Maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.280
was better than MapQuest and Yahoo Maps, for&nbsp;
modern standards, it was still rather slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.160
so their number one goal with the rewrite was to&nbsp;
maximize speed and optimize the user experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:56.480
After about an year, they would get the platform&nbsp;
to be nearly instantaneous, allowing them to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.840
much more ambitious moves starting with Satellite&nbsp;
view. Google would stitch together all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:07.080
imagery that they got from the Keyhole acquisition&nbsp;
to create satellite view. Fun fact, satellite view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:13.160
isn’t actually satellite view in most cases. It’s&nbsp;
actually arial view taken from low flying planes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:18.280
The Google team was actually debating on whether&nbsp;
to call it arial mode or satellite mode. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:23.160
Sergey Brin made the decision to call it Bird&nbsp;
Mode, the rest of the team would decide to unite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.840
and defy Sergey by just calling it Satellite Mode.&nbsp;
Apparently, Sergey would never comment on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:35.360
name change. But anyway, Satellite Mode would&nbsp;
come out in late 2005 and this was immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.720
a selling feature for Google Maps. Putting the&nbsp;
actual utility Satellite Mode aside, most users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.200
just liked messing around with it. Being able&nbsp;
to see your house and city from a birds eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:51.280
perspective was super neat. You kind of felt like&nbsp;
you were top secret spy or something. Google would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:57.880
simply take this novelty to the next level in May&nbsp;
29, 2007 with the debut of Street View. Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:03.680
View was obviously an extremely ambitious project.&nbsp;
Google was basically signing up to bankroll cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:09.720
driving around every single street in the entire&nbsp;
world. It turned out to be really popular feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.840
though. Now, not only could you see your city from&nbsp;
an arial perspective, but you could literally walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.720
through it as if you were actually there. But&nbsp;
while satellite mode and street view definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:25.440
made Google Maps unique and drove popularity, the&nbsp;
number one trend that made Google Maps ubiquitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.360
was of course smartphones. In fact, smartphone&nbsp;
based revenue accounts for nearly 90% of Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.720
Map’s revenue which is not that surprising.&nbsp;
Android is the most popular operating system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:42.480
in the world used by billions of people. And you&nbsp;
know what comes preinstalled on Android devices?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.920
It’s of course Google Maps. So, most Android users&nbsp;
are naturally just gonna use Google Maps whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.360
it’s the best or not just like how everyone used&nbsp;
to use Yahoo Maps and Mapquest. All of this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.640
allowed Google Maps to reach over 1 billion&nbsp;
monthly active users. But, while they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.160
eventually able to get to a dominant position,&nbsp;
it was by no means an easy road. While Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.200
was able to acquire the core 3 companies that&nbsp;
make up Google Maps for less than a $100 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.960
actually making Google Maps successful cost a lot&nbsp;
more money. According to one professor, just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:21.560
upfront cars, cameras, computers, and manpower&nbsp;
alone cost over $400 million. And Google never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.080
stopped updating their imagery. If anything, they&nbsp;
actually do it more and more often with time. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.040
there’s no question that creating and maintaining&nbsp;
Google Maps has cost Google billions of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:41.260
over the years which brings us to the question of&nbsp;
the day: how did they make all of this worthwhile?

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:46.880
MONETIZATION:
Google’s monetization strategy when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.520
comes to Maps breaksdown into 2 main categories.&nbsp;
Given that Google is an advertising company at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.840
end of the day, I don’t think you’d be surprised&nbsp;
to hear that the first category is ads, but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.840
actually done in a really tasteful helpful&nbsp;
way. You know when you pan around Google Maps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:08.360
you see businesses like Walmart and Home Depot and&nbsp;
Starbucks? Well, listing a business or location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:13.240
on Google Maps is actually completely free and&nbsp;
basically anyone can do it provided you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:18.280
right documents and rights. If you wanna take this&nbsp;
to the next level though with custom branding and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:24.160
high visibility, you can pay Google a fee and&nbsp;
basically make your listing a premium listing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:29.600
To be honest, this seems like a win win scenario.&nbsp;
Users are able to find businesses more easily with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:34.280
their logos and companies are able to increase&nbsp;
their brand presence in a discrete non intrusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:40.400
way. But that’s only half the story. Google’s&nbsp;
other mode of monetization is honestly far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.240
interesting which is through APIs. You know how&nbsp;
when you go to the website of a restaurant or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:50.360
local bowling alley, you’ll sometimes see a small&nbsp;
map showing you the location of the avenue? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:55.840
companies are able to do this thanks to Google&nbsp;
APIs for which they have to pay. And that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:02.000
more of a small use case. Imagine what happens&nbsp;
when someone like Fedex, Uber, AirBnB, Zillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:08.280
or DoorDash implements Google Maps. All I can&nbsp;
say is that that would cost quite a bit of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.960
and that’s just the most obvious implementation&nbsp;
of Google Maps API. Likely the most commonly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.680
used Google Maps API actually has nothing&nbsp;
to do with Maps directly. You know how when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.640
you start entering in a billing address or a&nbsp;
shipping address, the address autofills? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.040
that’s thanks to Google Places API. Basically&nbsp;
every company in the entire world who collects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:33.920
address information uses Google Places or a direct&nbsp;
competitor like Microsoft’s Azure Maps. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:39.720
don’t, they’re probably stuck in the 1990s. On&nbsp;
a per use basis, Google Places is relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:45.600
cheap. It’s only about 3 cents per use but think&nbsp;
about how many times you yourself might use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:52.480
every year. Maybe 10 times? That itself is 30&nbsp;
cents worth of revenue for Google. If 3 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.360
people were to do this every year, that itself&nbsp;
is $1 billion worth of revenue for Google from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.720
just one API. I think you can start to see how&nbsp;
all of this can add up to $11 billion when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:09.080
throw in hundreds of different APIs and uses&nbsp;
cases. But even putting the financials aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:14.680
thanks to Google Maps, Google has been able to&nbsp;
map out the entire globe in pinpoint detail. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:20.200
means knowing the exact address and location of&nbsp;
any and every commerical and residential property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:27.640
in the entire world. And if that wasn’t enough,&nbsp;
they know the exact location of billions of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:34.480
on this highly detailed map at any given point&nbsp;
in time. If that isn’t an insane amount of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:39.800
I don’t know what is. And that’s why even if&nbsp;
Google Maps never even made a single dime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:45.920
Google would be the real winner when it comes to&nbsp;
Google Maps. That fact that Google Maps pulls in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:52.400
$11 billion in revenue on top of that is simply&nbsp;
a bonus. With historically high interest rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

